K8s Interview Series – Part 5: Kubelet – The Node Guardian

Master the Kubelet with 15 essential interview questions! The Kubelet is the critical node agent that keeps your pods running.

1️⃣ Where does the Kubelet run?
On every worker node as the primary node agent managing pods and containers. It communicates with the API Server to receive pod specifications.

2️⃣ What is the Kubelet?
A system daemon that monitors pod lifecycle, typically managed as a systemd service. In managed K8s (EKS, GKE, AKS), cloud providers handle it.

3️⃣ What's the Kubelet's main job?
Ensures containers in PodSpecs are running and healthy. It receives instructions from API Server and delegates to CRI (Container Runtime Interface).

4️⃣ How does Kubelet communicate?
Only with API Server via REST API (HTTPS). It pulls Pod definitions, sends status updates, and authenticates with certificates/tokens.

5️⃣ Does Kubelet talk to etcd or scheduler?
❌ Never. Only communicates with API Server as the single hub.

6️⃣ What happens when Kubelet fails?
Node becomes "NotReady". Node Controller detects missed heartbeats. Existing containers may run but won't be monitored.

7️⃣ How are pods replaced on failed nodes?
Controllers (ReplicaSet, Deployment) create replacement pods. Scheduler places them on healthy nodes.

8️⃣ How does Kubelet manage containers?
Uses CRI for lifecycle management: start, stop, health checks. Runtimes: containerd, CRI-O.

9️⃣ How does Kubelet handle networking?
Uses CNI (Container Network Interface) to assign pod IPs and configure routes. Examples: Calico, Cilium, AWS VPC CNI.

🔟 Where does Kubelet read PodSpecs?
Two sources:
API Server (standard)
Static manifests from /etc/kubernetes/manifests/

1️⃣1️⃣ What are the three K8s probes?
Startup: Checks app start Liveness: Checks if alive (restarts if fails) Readiness: Checks if ready for traffic

1️⃣2️⃣ How does Kubelet use probes?
Performs periodic checks from PodSpec. Restarts unhealthy containers based on restartPolicy. Syncs desired vs actual state.

1️⃣3️⃣ What are Static Pods?
Pods created directly by Kubelet without scheduler. Used for control-plane components (kube-apiserver, etcd). Lives in /etc/kubernetes/manifests/.

1️⃣4️⃣ What metrics does Kubelet report?
Pod/container status (Running, Pending, Failed)
Resource usage (CPU, memory, disk)
Event logs & probe results

1️⃣5️⃣ What are Kubelet restart policies?
Always: Restart on any exit OnFailure: Restart only on error Never: No restart

   🎯 Key Takeaway

Kubelet is the node guardian ensuring containers run as specified. It's the bridge between control plane (API Server) and actual workload execution (CRI runtime).


K8s Interview Series – Part 6: kube-proxy – The Network Traffic Controller

1️⃣ What is kube-proxy?
A network proxy running on every worker node that implements the Kubernetes Service abstraction. It maintains network rules to enable communication between Services and Pods.

2️⃣ Where does kube-proxy run?
On every worker node only (not control plane). Deployed as a DaemonSet ensuring one instance per node. 

3️⃣ Is kube-proxy a process, service, or daemon?
A system daemon deployed as either a DaemonSet pod (modern K8s) or systemd service (bare-metal/VM setups). Runs continuously to maintain network rules.

4️⃣ What is kube-proxy's main function?
Maintains network rules (iptables/IPVS) that route traffic from Service IPs (ClusterIP/NodePort) to actual Pod IPs. Provides load balancing across pod replicas automatically.

5️⃣ How does kube-proxy communicate with control plane?
Watches the API Server for changes to Services and Endpoints objects. It's a one-way communication (read-only)—kube-proxy never writes data back to API Server.

6️⃣ Does kube-proxy handle pod-to-pod networking?
❌ No! Pod-to-pod networking is handled by CNI plugins (Calico, Cilium, Flannel). kube-proxy only handles Service-to-Pod traffic routing.

7️⃣ What are the three kube-proxy modes?
userspace: Legacy, slow (traffic goes through userspace) iptables: Default mode, uses kernel netfilter rules IPVS: High-performance mode using Linux kernel load balancer

8️⃣ How does iptables mode work?
Creates iptables rules in the NAT table that intercept packets destined for Service IPs and rewrite them to backend Pod IPs. Uses random selection for load balancing.

9️⃣ What is IPVS mode and why use it?
IP Virtual Server - kernel-level load balancer offering better performance than iptables for clusters with 1000+ Services. Supports multiple algorithms (round-robin, least-connection, etc.).

🔟 What happens when kube-proxy fails?
Existing connections may continue temporarily, but new Service connections fail. Services become unreachable as network rules stop updating. Pods can still communicate using direct Pod IPs.

1️⃣1️⃣ How does kube-proxy implement ClusterIP?
Creates network rules mapping ClusterIP:ServicePort to PodIP:ContainerPort. Traffic is distributed across healthy backend pods using round-robin (iptables) or selected algorithm (IPVS).

1️⃣2️⃣ How does NodePort work with kube-proxy?
Opens a specific port (30000-32767 range) on all worker nodes. Traffic to any NodeIP:NodePort is forwarded to Service backend pods, enabling external cluster access.

1️⃣3️⃣ Does kube-proxy manage LoadBalancer Services?
Partially. Cloud controller provisions the external LB (AWS ELB, GCP LB). kube-proxy handles internal routing from node to pods after traffic enters the cluster.

1️⃣4️⃣ Difference between kube-proxy and CNI?
CNI: Assigns Pod IPs, handles pod-to-pod direct communication 
kube-proxy: Implements Service abstraction, handles Service-to-Pod routing and load balancing.
